# High Initial Reversible Capacity and Long Life of Ternary SnO2-Co-carbon Nanocomposite Anodes for Lithium-Ion Batteries

## abstract
The two major limitations in the application of SnO2 for lithium-ion battery (LIB) anodes are the large volume variations of SnO2 during repeated lithiation/delithiation processes and a large irreversible capacity loss during the first cycle, which can lead to a rapid capacity fade and unsatisfactory initial Coulombic efficiency (ICE). To overcome these limitations, we developed composites of ultrafine SnO2 nanoparticles and in situ formed Co(CoSn) nanocrystals embedded in an N-doped carbon matrix using a Co-based metal-organic framework (ZIF-67). The formed Co additives and structural advantages of the carbon-confined SnO2/Co nanocomposite effectively inhibited Sn coarsening in the lithiated SnO2 and mitigated its structural degradation while facilitating fast electronic transport and facile ionic diffusion. As a result, the electrodes demonstrated high ICE (82.2%), outstanding rate capability (~ 800 mAh g-1 at a high current density of 5 A g-1), and long-term cycling stability (~ 760 mAh g-1 after 400 cycles at a current density of 0.5 A g-1). This study will be helpful in developing high-performance Si (Sn)-based oxide, Sn/Sb-based sulfide, or selenide electrodes for LIBs. In addition, some metal organic frameworks similar to ZIF-67 can also be used as composite templates.

High Initial Reversible Capacity and Long Life of Ternary $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ ‑Co‑carbon Nanocomposite Anodes for Lithium‑Ion Batteries
## HIGHLIGHTS
• $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -Co-carbon nanocomposites were in-situ prepared from Co-based metal–organic frameworks and showed a remarkably high initial Coulombic efficiency $(82.2\%)$ and a capacity of $\sim800\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a high current density of ${\mathfrak{s}}\operatorname{A}\operatorname{g}^{-1}$ .
•	 Facile approach for designing highly reversible and stable electrodes for next-generation high-performance lithium-ion batteries.
ABSTRACT  The two major limitations in the application of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ for lithium-ion battery (LIB) anodes are the large volume variations of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ during repeated lithiation/delithiation processes and a large irreversible capacity loss during the first cycle, which can lead to a rapid capacity fade and unsatisfactory initial Coulombic efficiency (ICE). To overcome these limitations, we developed composites of ultrafine $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles and in situ formed $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{Co}\mathrm{{Sn})}$ nanocrystals embedded in an N-doped carbon matrix using a Co-based metal–organic framework (ZIF-67). The formed $\mathrm{Co}$ additives and structural advantages of the carbon-confined $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}$ nanocomposite effectively inhibited Sn coarsening in the lithiated $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and mitigated its structural degradation while facilitating fast electronic transport and facile ionic diffusion. As a result, the electrodes demonstrated high ICE $(82.2\%)$ , outstanding rate capability $(\sim800\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a high current density of ${\mathfrak{s}}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ), and long-term cycling stability $(\sim760\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 400 cycles at a current density of $0.5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ). This study will be helpful in developing high-performance Si (Sn)-based oxide, $\mathrm{{Sn/Sb}}$ -based sulfide, or selenide electrodes for LIBs. In addition, some metal organic frameworks similar to ZIF-67 can also be used as composite templates.
KEYWORDS  Ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanostructures; ZIF-67 frameworks; Enhanced initial Coulombic efficiency; Reversible conversion reaction
## 1  Introduction
Over the past few years, the applications of lithium-ion bat‑ teries (LIBs) have extended from consumer electronics to power batteries. This impressive progress achieved in this field suggests that LIBs will continue to be a dominant power source for electric vehicles in the next decade [1]. In the pursuit of further improvement of LIBs, various efforts have been made to rationalize their design and to develop advanced electrode materials with high specific capacity, prolonged life span, and good rate capability [2, 3]. Graph‑ ite is the most widely used LIB anode. However, it exhibits a limited specific capacity of $372\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ . Therefore, in 2011, Sony Corporation produced novel LIBs (Nexelion). Specifically, their anodes consisted of Sn-Co–C compos‑ ites. Various tin-based anodes have also been fabricated in order to develop high-performance LIBs [2–4]. Among these anodes, tin oxide $(\mathrm{SnO}_{2})$ anodes have been extensively stud‑ ied because $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ can store ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ via a two-step reaction and shows a high theoretical specific capacity of $1494\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ storage of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , the first step is the conversion reaction $(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}+4\mathrm{Li}\xrightarrow{}\mathrm{Sn}+2\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , which generates a capacity of $731\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ . In the subsequent lithiation/del‑ ithiation process, an alloying reaction $\mathrm{(Sn+4.4Li\mathrm{\-}L i_{4.4}S n)}$ ) occurs, delivering a capacity of $763\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ [6–8]. How‑ ever, there are two major challenges in developing $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ anodes with a high specific capacity: (i) capacity loss induced by the huge volume variations (greater than $300\%$ ) generated during cycling, (ii) the irreversibility of the con‑ version reaction, which reduces the initial Coulombic effi‑ ciency (ICE) of the anode [5–7].
Various attempts have been made to overcome these limitations. For example, various carbon-based $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ com‑ posites have been developed to accommodate the volume expansions caused by cycling in order to achieve cycling stability [8–18]. However, this carbon composite approach cannot improve the ICE of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ anodes. Hu et al. reported that $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ electrodes with Sn grains $<11\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diam‑ eter show a completely reversible conversion reaction $\mathrm{(Sn+Li_{2}O\to S n O_{2})}$ ) [19]. Transition metals $\mathrm{{(}M=C u}$ , Fe, Mn, Co, etc.) or metal oxides are used to stabilize nanostruc‑ tured $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and can also improve the reversibility of the con‑ version reaction between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ [4, 20–25]. Inactive metals can buffer the expansion of $\mathrm{\bfSn}$ particles (to coarsen them) and migrate to the $\mathrm{Sn}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ surface so that $\mathrm{Sn}$ can remain active with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Thus, the introduction of transition metals can improve the ICE of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ electrodes. To sum up, there are mainly three mainstream solutions for develop‑ ing high-performance $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based anodes: (1) designing a unique carbon-based structure including the surface coating to suppress the full volume expansions [26, 27] while pro‑ viding an expansion space [8, 28–31], (2) preparing ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles to aggrandize the grain boundaries and alleviate the mechanical strain and improve the reversibility of the conversion reaction [6], and (3) introducing transition metals or forming intermetallic alloys to make the conver‑ sion reaction reversible and mitigate the expansion of Sn simultaneously [4, 20–23].
Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with inor‑ ganic (Co and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ) and organic molecules have been used as a novel 3D porous carbon source for developing adjust‑ able templates to anchor guest transition metals [32–34]. In this study, we fabricated a novel ternary $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{nO}_{2}$ -Co-C composite by mixing ultrafine $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles with a Co-based MOF (ZIF-67) (denoted as N-u-SCC) to develop LIB anodes with high ICE and long-term cycle stability. ZIF-67 serves as a sacrificial template for the formation of Co additives and 3D porous carbon frameworks. This welldesigned structure showed the advantages of the unique 3D carbon-based nanostructure with in situ formed Co additives and suppressed the volume expansions and Sn coarsening of the lithiated $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . This improved the cycling performance of the anodes and rendered the conversion reaction highly reversible. The 3D porous carbon framework served as an excellent carrier for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (to be anchored) and improved the conductivity of the entire composite while providing enough space for volume variations during the lithiation/delithiation process. The in situ formed Co additives not only prevented the covering of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ by $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and alleviated the volume expansions, but also served as good electron conductors. As a result, the N-u-SCC-2 electrode showed a high ICE of $82.2\%$ (average level). In addition, the electrodes showed extraordinary specific capacity $(\sim975\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at $0.2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1},$ , high capacity retention ( $78.6\%$ after 100 cycles at $0.2\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), excellent rate capability (a revers‑ ible capacity of $\sim800\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ under the current density of $5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ , and prolonged life span.
## 2  Experimental Section
All the chemicals used in this work were analytically pure and were commercially available. Commercial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , 2-methylimidazole were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co. Ltd. ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{3}$ and urea were purchased from Xilong Scientific.
## 2.1  Synthesis of Ultrafine $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$
Ultrafine $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ was prepared by modifying the method reported by Lou et al. [3]. In a typical reaction, $2.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{NaSnO}_{3}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $16\mathrm{mmol}$ of urea were added into a solution of $145~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and the resulting mixture was stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The reaction mix‑ ture was then transferred to a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave, which was heated in an oven at $190^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{h}$ . The reaction mixture was centrifuged to obtain precipitates, which were dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight and annealed at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ .
## 2.2  Synthesis of ZIF‑67 Frameworks
## 2.3  Synthesis of N‑c‑SCC and N‑u‑SCC Composites
The N-doped commercial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -Co-C (denoted as N-c-SCC) and N-u-SCC composites were prepared using a method similar to that used for the preparation of the ZIF-67 frame‑ works. The only difference was that $0.2~\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ (com‑ mercial or ultrafine) was added into solution A followed by sonication for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the sonication, solution A was stirred for another $10\mathrm{min}$ for better dispersion. Then, solu‑ tion B was quickly added to solution A and the resulting mixture was stirred vigorously for another $3\mathrm{min}$ . The reac‑ tion mixture was then static aged for $22\mathrm{{h}}$ . The precipitates separated from the solution by centrifugation were freezedried and then annealed at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.4  Material Characterization
The morphology of the as-prepared samples was examined by a SUPRA 55 field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) exami‑ nations were carried out on a JEOL JEM 2100F at $200\mathrm{kV}.$ . The elemental mapping and energy-dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spec‑ troscopy (EDS) measurements of the samples were taken on an energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometer equipped with the JEOL 2100F microscope. The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were recorded on a Rigaku Ultima IV with $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K\upalpha}$ radiation $(\lambda=0.15418\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was carried out using an Axis Ultra DLD spectrometer.
## 2.5  Electrochemical Measurements
The electrochemical performance of the as-prepared com‑ posites was evaluated using R2032 coin-type half cells assembled in a glove box filled with argon. The oxygen and moisture contents of the glove box were $<0.5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . The electrodes were prepared by confecting a slurry containing the active materials, carbon black, and carboxymethyl cel‑ lulose with a ratio of 7:2:1 in a solution of water and ethanol. This slurry solution was stirred for $^{8\mathrm{{h}}}$ and was then casted onto a $\mathrm{cu}$ foil and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ under vacuum. The active material loading on each electrode was $0.9\mathrm{-}1.1~\mathrm{mg}$ . A solution of $1\mathrm{MLiPF}_{6}$ in ethylene carbonate and diethyl carbonate (at a volume ratio of 1:2) containing $10\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ fluoroethylene carbonate (FEC) was used as the electrolyte.
The electrochemical measurements of the electrodes were taken using a Neware battery tester over the potential range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{V}$ . The specific capacity of the composites was calculated using their whole masses. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were taken on an electrochemical work‑ station $\left(\mathbf{CHI660C}\right)$ over the voltage range of $0.01{-}3\mathrm{~V~}$ at a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ .
## 3  Results and Discussion
Figure  1 shows the schematic of the synthesis of N-uSCC. The as-prepared ultrafine $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles and $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ were uniformly dispersed in an alcoholic solution. To this solution, an alcoholic solution of 2-methyl‑ imidazole (2-Melm) was added to generate the $\operatorname{SnO}_{2}@Z\operatorname{IF}.$ - 67 composite. After annealing under an inert atmosphere,
ZIF-67 carbonized and its structure collapsed. This eventu‑ ally resulted in the formation of carbon frameworks with metallic Co. We also prepared composites using commercial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . For this, commercial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was added to the alco‑ holic solution during the synthesis of ZIF-67. The reaction mixture was then heat-treated under an inert atmosphere to finally obtain the in situ formed N-c-SCC. Table S1 lists the synthetic formulae and ICE values of the three N-c-SCC electrodes. The morphology of commercial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and the three N-c-SCC composites is shown in Fig. S1.
The phase composition of the samples was analyzed using their XRD patterns. The XRD patterns shown in Fig. 2a reveal that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (JCPDS No. 77-0447) was the main phase of all the three samples. With an increase in the carbon con‑ tent, the CoSn phase of the samples became predominant. No other impurity was detected. Figure 2b shows the ICE of the pure commercial $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ and N-c-SCC electrodes. The N-c-SCC electrodes showed better electrochemical perfor‑ mance than the pure commercial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ electrode. The ICE of the N-c-SCC electrodes increased from 62.6 to $72.5\%$ . This is because Co nanoparticles improved the reversibility of the conversion reaction on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . Taking the variate into consideration, this remarkable improvement in the ICE of the N-c-SCC electrodes was due to the ZIF-67 frameworks, which consisted only of Co and carbon. This demonstrates the viability of our design, in which ZIF-67 was used as the sacrificial template. To further evaluate the electrochemical performance of the four electrodes, their charge–discharge curves were plotted, as shown in Fig. S2. The charging potential of all the $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ electrodes could be divided into three regions, each of which involved different reaction processes. Similarly, the capacity of the electrodes in each cycle could be divided into three parts: $0.01{-}1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ corre‑ sponding to the dealloying reaction $(\mathrm{Li}_{x}\mathrm{Sn}\longrightarrow\mathrm{Sn}),$ ), $1.0{-}2.4\:\mathrm{V}$ corresponding to the conversion reaction $(\mathrm{Sn}\longrightarrow\mathrm{SnO}_{2})$ , and $2.4\mathrm{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ corresponding to some other reactions like the consumption of the electrolyte [25]. On the basis of the electrochemical measurements and morphological analysis of the electrodes, it can be stated that (1) the contact area between Co and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ significantly improves the reversibility of the conversion reaction. At the N-u-SCC-1, the highest capacity was observed in all the cycles over the potential range of $1.0{-}2.4\:\mathrm{V}.$ (2) An increase in the carbon content improved the reversibility of the conversion reaction and the cycling stability of the electrodes. Figure S2 reveals that the N-c-SCC-3 electrode showed the best cycling stability.
To further optimize the electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ electrodes, commercial $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ was substituted by the asprepared ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $(\sim15\mathrm{nm})$ ). The N-doped ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -Co-C composites (denoted as N-u-SCC) were prepared using a method same as that used for preparing the N-c-SCC composites. Table 1 lists the synthetic formulae, carbon contents, and Sn and Co weight ratios of the two N-u-SCC composites. The morphology of the ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles and N-u-SCC-1 and N-u-SCC-2 electrodes was observed. Figure 3a, d shows that the ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nano‑ particles had a diameter of $\sim15\ \mathrm{nm}$ and were evenly distrib‑ uted in the composites. The morphology and diameter of the frameworks of N-u-SCC-1 (Figs. 3b, e) were similar to those shown in Fig. S1c. This can be attributed to the same $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/2$ -Melm ratio (1:8) in both the cases. Since the ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ particles were much smaller than commer‑ cial $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ particles, they offered a larger contact area with metallic Co. At the $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O/2}$ -Melm ratio of 1:16, the frameworks became less stable and could be hardly observed. However, carbon was predominant, as shown in Fig. 3c, f.
The morphology of the N-u-SCC-2 composite was fur‑ ther analyzed by TEM. Figure 4a shows a typical TEM image of the as- prepared N-u-SCC-2 composite. It can be observed that the carbon frameworks consisted of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and Co (CoSn) nanoparticles (light zone), which allevi‑ ated the volume variations during the subsequent dein‑ tercalation reactions. The sufficient contact area between $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and Co (CoSn) improved the reversibility of the conversion reaction, thus improving the ICE and cycling performance of the electrode. The lattice distances of 0.32 and $0.24{\mathrm{~nm}}$ (Fig. 4b) correspond to the (110) and (200) planes of crystalline $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . The lattice distance of $0.22{\mathrm{nm}}$ corresponds to the (111) lattice plane of CoSn. The lat‑ tice distances of Sn could not be detected. This can be attributed to the low Sn content of the nanocomposite and the similarity in the lattice distances of $\mathrm{Sn}$ and $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . Figure 4c shows the elemental mapping of the N-u-SCC-2 composite under ultrahigh magnification. This figure con‑ firms that Sn and Co were uniformly distributed in the carbon frameworks and showed sufficient contact. The Sn and Co weight ratios of N-u-SCC-2 were about $50.8\%$ and $11.2\%$ , respectively, while those of N-u-SCC-1 were $51.5\%$ and $25.1\%$ , respectively, as revealed by the EDS results (Table S2).
The phase composition of the ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ particles and N-u-SCC-1 ­( $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -ratio 1 in Fig. 5a) and N-u-SCC-2 com‑ posites ­ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -ratio 2 in Fig. 5a) was analyzed using their XRD patterns. The XRD peaks of ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ could be indexed to JCPDS No. 77-0447. Like commercial $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , both
The valence states of Sn and $\mathbf{\mathrm{Co}}$ in the N-u-SCC-2 com‑ posite were analyzed by XPS. As can be observed from Fig. 5c, Sn showed a main valence of $S\mathrm{n}^{4+}$ . This is consist‑ ent with the observation that $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ was the main phase of this composite. In addition, some Sn present on the sur‑ face showed a valence of zero. This can be attributed to the reduction of metallic Sn by carbon and Co during the annealing process (as observed from the XRD patterns). Oxygen vacancies were generated at this point. The Co $2p$ spectra (Fig. 5d) of the near-surface region of the compos‑ ite showed three types of valence (Co, CoO, and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ . The increasing valence could be assigned to the oxidation of metallic Co. The small amount of oxidation in the near surface of the composite could not be detected by XRD. Figure 5e, f shows the $\mathrm{~N~}1s$ and $\mathrm{~C~}1s$ spectra of the N-uSCC-2 composite and confirms the presence of N and C in it. Moreover, the high-resolution $\textbf{N}1s$ XPS spectra showed that the composite consisted of pyridinic N ${\mathrm{~/~}}(398.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ and pyrrolic N $(400.5\mathrm{eV})$ (Fig. S5). The pyridinic N and pyr‑ rolic $\mathbf{N}$ contents of this composite were $80.3\%$ and $19.7\%$ , respectively (Fig. S5). These results indicate the N-doped C composite $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was the main active phase of the N-uSCC-2 composite.
Figure 6a shows the CV curves of the N-u-SCC-2 elec‑ trode. The peak observed at ${\sim}0.87\mathrm{~V~}$ during the first cycle can be attributed to the reduction of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ to metallic Sn or $\mathrm{\DeltaSnO}$ and the formation of solid–electrolyte interface (SEI) layers, which accounted for the disappearing of this peak in the subsequent cycles. The relatively large area of this peak indicates that a large amount of reaction/layer was formed. The peaks observed at around 0.3 and $0.2\mathrm{V}$ in the subsequent cycles correspond to a series of Li–Sn alloying reactions. The anodic peaks at 0.50, 0.61, 0.73, and $0.78{\mathrm{V}}$ can be attributed to the $\operatorname{Li}_{x}\operatorname{Sn}\to\operatorname{Sn}$ dealloying reactions. This is consistent with the differential charge capacity ver‑ sus voltage curves obtained in the subsequent cycles. The broad peaks at 1.25 and $2.06\mathrm{V}$ correspond to the reversible oxidation of metallic Sn to $\mathrm{{\calSnO}}$ and $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ , respectively. The peaks remained stable and could be clearly observed dur‑ ing the 4th cycle, demonstrating the high reversibility of the conversion reactions. After the first cycle, the curves showed similar peak patterns, indicating that the stability of the electrode increased gradually. Figure 6b shows the first three discharge/charge curves of the N-u-SCC-2 electrode at a current density of $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . The initial discharge capac‑ ity of the electrode was $1365.2\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ . Similar curves were obtained in the subsequent cycles. This attests the high ICE and excellent cycling performance of the electrode. For unveiling the reversible nature of the conversion reaction, the differential charge capacity versus voltage curves of the N-u-SCC-2 electrode at the 1st, 10th, 50th, and 100th cycles were obtained (Fig. 6c). The peaks observed over the potential range of $0.01{-}1.0\mathrm{V}$ correspond to the dealloying reactions ­ $\mathrm{Li}_{x}\mathrm{Sn}$ to Sn), while the peaks at 1.0 to $-2.4\mathrm{~V~}$ correspond to the conversion reactions (Sn to $\mathrm{\DeltaSnO}$ or $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) after 100 cycles. The peaks (integral intensities and poten‑ tial positions) corresponding to the conversion reactions remained the same (1st and 2nd cycles) even after the 50th cycle. These results demonstrate the high reversibility of the conversion reaction.
The N-u-SCC-2 electrode showed a reversible discharge capacity of $\sim975\ \mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles (Fig. 6d) at a current density of $0.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , which corresponds to a capac‑ ity retention ratio of $78.6\%$ (when compared with the 2nd cycle). On the other hand, the N-u-SCC-1 and pure $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ electrodes exhibited much smaller reversible discharge capacities of 504 and $226.0\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, after 100 cycles. The first discharge capacities of the N-u-SCC-2, N-u-SCC-1, and pure $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ electrodes were 1365.2, 990.5, and $1736.7\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. This high capacity reten‑ tion of the N-u-SCC-2 electrode can be attributed to its high carbon content, which provided more space for volume vari‑ ations along with a larger $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Sn/Co}$ contact area to inhibit the volume expansion, thus increasing the cycling life of the electrode. It should be noted that the N-u-SCC-2 electrode showed an average ICE of $82.2\%$ because of the formation of SEI layers. This improved the reversibility of the reactions between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and Li. On the other hand, the N-u-SCC-1 and pure $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ electrodes showed an ICE of $68.1\%$ and $59.7\%$ , respectively (Fig. S6). The N-u-SCC-2 electrode exhibited smaller internal resistance than the other two electrodes, as revealed by the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements shown in Fig. S7a. The EIS measure‑ ments of the N-u-SCC-2 electrode after 100 cycles were also taken. An equivalent circuit consisting of the resistances of the electrolyte $(R_{\mathrm{e}})$ , charge transfer $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , and constant phase elements and Warburg impedance was proposed to fit the impedance data. Low $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ values were obtained after 100 cycles $(179-32\Omega)$ ), suggesting the enhanced charge transfer kinetics (Table S4). In addition, the N-u-SCC-2 electrode showed excellent rate capability. Figure S8a shows the dis‑ charge/charge curves of the electrode at different current densities. All the curves showed similar trend with the same discharge/charge platform. Furthermore, a reversible capac‑ ity of ${\sim}800\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ was obtained when the current density was increased to ${\mathfrak{s}}\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . At the current densities of 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, and ${\mathfrak{s}}\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , discharge capacities of \~ 1500, 1240, 1090, 965, and $800\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, were obtained. As the current returned to $0.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , the capacity became stable, as shown in Fig. S8b. This extraordinary rate capability of N-uSCC-2 was not due to its unique nanostructure consisting only of $\mathrm{Co}$ and carbon framework, but can be attributed to the oxygen vacancies, which improved the conductivity and transportation of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}[28,31,35{-}40]$ . The N-u-SCC-2 elec‑ trode exhibited a cycling life of up to 450 cycles and main‑ tained a reversible capacity of ${\sim}760\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at the current density of $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , as shown in Fig. 6e. After 150 cycles, the diffusion kinetics of lithium ions improved after the ini‑ tial activation. The optimization of the SEI layer at the ini‑ tial stage leads to capacity fading because of its breakdown and reconstruction. The cycling performance of an electrode improves after the formation of a stable SEI layer. The longterm cycling performance of the N-u-SCC-2 electrodes at relatively high current densities of $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}}$ for 200 cycles and $2\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ for 300 cycles was also evaluated, as shown in Fig. S9. As can be observed from the figure, the electrode exhibited a desirable long-term cycling performance.
We also compared the ICE of the N-u-SCC-2 anode with that of previously reported $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ LIB anodes. (Materials for LIBs are listed in Table S3.) The N-u-SCC-2 anode showed the highest ICE and outstanding electrochemical properties. Figure 7 shows the schematic of the delithiation process of pure $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ , and N-u-SCC electrodes. As shown in Fig. 7a, after lithiation, $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ particles became larger and the distances between them decreased. This resulted in the gradual agglomeration of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles, leading to poor cycling life and low ICE of the electrode. Upon the incorporation of carbon materials like graphene and 3D frameworks (Fig. 7b), these $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ particles scat‑ tered and became more dispersive in the original state after being coated with carbon. The introduced carbon materials suppressed the volume variations of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ particles during lithiation, provided extra space for volume variations, and served as barriers to prevent the aggregation of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ parti‑ cles so that the particles were still dispersive after delithia‑ tion. Similarly, the N-u-SCC-2 composite consisted of 3D carbon frameworks, which prevented the aggregation and volume variations of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ particles during lithiation. How‑ ever, the 3D carbon frameworks in this composite consisted of uniformly distributed Co (CoSn) particles. Co does not participate in the delithiation reactions and remains stable. This makes Co a powerful barrier to prevent the volume var‑ iations and particle agglomeration of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . Thus, the exqui‑ sitely designed N-u-SCC-2 composite showed extraordinary electrochemical properties. Figure S10 shows the FESEM images of the ultrafine $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ particles and N-u-SCC-2 com‑ posites after 100 cycles. Pure $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ grains showed severe aggregation (Fig. S10b), while N-u-SCC-2 exhibited little aggregation and a morphology similar to that observed ini‑ tially (Fig. S10d). Figure S10e, f shows the TEM images of the original N-u-SCC-2 and N-u-SCC-2 electrodes after discharging to $1.0\mathrm{V}.$ The morphology remained the same except that the latter became amorphous. These observations demonstrate the potential of the N-u-SCC-2 composite for application as an LIB anode.
## 4  Conclusion
In summary, a novel ternary $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}–\mathbf{Co-C}$ nanocomposite (N-u-SCC) was successfully prepared via a simple and lowcost synthesis method. In this design, the in situ formation of Co additives from the Co-based ZIF-67 framework ren‑ dered the $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ conversion reaction highly reversible and the N-doped carbon frameworks efficiently mitigated the structural degradation of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ while facilitating electronic transport and ionic diffusion. Accordingly, the optimized N-u-SCC electrodes exhibited excellent electrochemical performance with high ICE (average $82.2\%$ ), outstanding rate performance $\mathrm{\800\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $5\mathrm{A~g^{-1}}{\cdot}$ ), and long-term cycling performance $(\sim760\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 400 cycles at a current density of $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\Omega}},$ ). These findings will be helpful for developing highly reversible and stable electrodes for next-generation high-performance LIBs.
